# Virtual Cloud Printer (Windows)

Create one or more **virtual printers** on Windows. Anything you print to them —
from any app (Word, Chrome, PDF viewers, your ERP, …) — is converted to a **PDF**
and **POSTed** together with the document name.

It runs in one of two modes:

- **LAN → LIMS pipeline (primary).** Offline client PCs print to a central **hub**
  on the LAN (`hub\`), which files every PDF into the shared
  `limsDocs\<device>\<device type>\<registration>\<test>\` tree and instantly
  forwards it to a cloud **LIMS** (Supabase + React, `lims\`). At print time the
  user picks the **registration number** and **test** from dropdowns synced from
  the LIMS — nothing typed by hand, and a job without a valid registration is
  **held** at the hub (never lost) until one is assigned.
  **📗 Deployment runbook: [LAN-SETUP.md](LAN-SETUP.md).**
- **Legacy direct-URL mode.** Each printer POSTs straight to its own HTTPS URL —
  exactly as before. Pass `-Url` (or answer the URL prompt) and nothing else changes.

```
Any app ─Print▶ "GCMS Printer" ─▶ pick Registration ▶ pick Test ─▶ PDF
      ─▶ hub (192.168.1.172:8000) ─▶ limsDocs\gcms-01\gcms\R-2026-0001\rx-test\…
                                   ─▶ cloud LIMS (appears live)
```

---

## Quick start

1. **Double-click `install.bat`** and approve the Administrator prompt (UAC).
2. A **click-through wizard** opens (Next → Next → Install; you can paste into
   every box): pick *LIMS hub printer* and fill the hub URL, unique **device
   name**, **enroll key** (from the hub console), **device type** and optional
   per-printer **PDF password** — see [LAN-SETUP.md](LAN-SETUP.md) — or pick
   *Legacy printer* and just give a URL.
3. Click **Install** and watch the progress in the window (first run downloads a
   few tools — see below).
4. Done. Open any app → Print → pick your printer. The job is uploaded as a PDF.

<sub>Prefer a console / scripting? `setup.ps1 -Action install` still has the
original prompt-based and fully-parameterized flows.</sub>

To add another printer later: **double-click `add-printer.bat`**.
To check things are working: **double-click `status.bat`**.
To remove everything: **double-click `uninstall.bat`**.

---

## Full setup on a desktop, from `git clone` to a working printer

> 📘 **For the complete, step-by-step walkthrough (prerequisites → clone →
> simulator → install → registration numbers → verify → troubleshoot → reset),
> see [SETUP.md](SETUP.md).** The condensed version follows.

On the target **Windows** machine:

1. **Install Git** (if needed) — <https://git-scm.com/download/win> — then clone:
   ```bat
   git clone https://github.com/arupa444/virtualPrinterMegsan.git
   cd virtualPrinterMegsan
   ```
   (Or download the repo ZIP from GitHub and extract it.)

2. *(Optional but recommended for a first run)* **Start the local simulator** so
   you can watch jobs arrive, in a separate terminal:
   ```bat
   cd simulator
   run.bat
   ```
   It prints an **admin token** and serves `http://localhost:8000/`. Open that
   page, paste the token, create a link, and copy its ingest URL
   (`http://localhost:8000/ingest/<token>`). See [simulator/README.md](simulator/README.md).

3. **Install the printer.** Back in the repo root, **double-click `install.bat`**
   (approve the UAC prompt). When prompted, enter:
   - a **printer name** (e.g. `Invoices`)
   - the **URL** — paste the simulator link from step 2, or your real HTTPS endpoint.

4. **Print.** Open any app → Print → choose your printer. The job is converted to
   PDF and POSTed. If you used the simulator, refresh its dashboard to see the PDF
   and document name; otherwise check your endpoint. `status.bat` and
   `%ProgramData%\VirtualCloudPrinter\log.txt` confirm what happened.

5. **Add more printers** anytime with `add-printer.bat`; **remove everything**
   with `uninstall.bat`.

> For confidential/production data, read
> [COMPLIANCE-AND-PRIVACY.md](COMPLIANCE-AND-PRIVACY.md) first — especially the
> Ghostscript licensing note and the HTTPS/`verify_tls` requirement.

## What gets installed (automatically, on first run)

The installer is self-contained; it fetches what it needs the first time:

| Component | Why | How it's obtained |
|---|---|---|
| **uv** + a private **Python 3.12** | runs the uploader | `winget` or the official uv installer; Python is placed under `%ProgramData%\VirtualCloudPrinter\python` |
| **Ghostscript** | converts PostScript → PDF | `winget` (`ArtifexSoftware.GhostScript`), or the official installer from GitHub |
| **mfilemon** print-port monitor | captures the print job and runs our uploader | silent install of `mfilemon-setup.exe` from the author's **GitHub releases** (or an offline copy in `vendor\`) |
| **qpdf** | AES-256 PDF encryption (only if you enable it) | GitHub release zip → `%ProgramData%\VirtualCloudPrinter\qpdf`, or `winget` / `vendor\qpdf-*.zip` |
| **MS Publisher (v3 PostScript) driver** | inbox driver that emits PostScript | already in Windows (`prnge001.inf`) |

Everything the tool itself creates lives in **`%ProgramData%\VirtualCloudPrinter\`**:
`upload.py`, `prompt-id.ps1`, `config.json`, the `venv`, `python`, `spool\`,
`failed\`, `ids\`, and `log.txt`.

> **Why a v3 driver?** A v4 *class* driver (e.g. "Microsoft PS Class Driver")
> cannot be attached to a port owned by a third-party port monitor (mfilemon) —
> Windows rejects it. The installer uses an inbox **v3 pscript5** driver, which
> binds cleanly and still emits PostScript.

> **Requirements:** Windows 10/11 (64-bit), Administrator rights for install, and
> internet access on first run to fetch the tools above.

---

## Test it locally first (recommended)

Before pointing at a real server, verify the whole pipeline on your own machine:

```bat
python test_server.py
```

It prints a URL like `http://localhost:8000/upload`. Set a printer to that URL
(either during install, or edit `config.json` — see below), then print anything.
The received PDF and the document name show up in the console and in `.\received\`.

---

## Attaching a registration number / UUID to a print job

The identifier is sent to your URL as its own form field, **`registration_number`**
(rename via `registration_field` in `config.json`), alongside `docname` + `file`.
There are three ways to supply it — no admin needed for any:

- **Automatic per-print prompt (default).** Each time you print, a dialog pops up
  on your screen. On a **hub printer** it shows two dropdowns — the open
  **registrations** for this printer's device type (searchable) and that
  registration's **tests** — fed live from the hub's catalog (with a
  `limsDocs\.vcp\catalog` share fallback); **Skip** sends the job anyway and the
  hub **holds** it until a registration is assigned in its dashboard. On a legacy
  printer it is the original free-text box (type a value or **New UUID**). Turn
  the prompt off with `"prompt_registration": false` in `config.json`.
- **Set Print ID (single, before printing).** Double-click **`set-id.bat`** (or the
  **"Set Print ID"** desktop shortcut), enter a number, **Save**, then print. Serves
  as the fallback when the prompt is skipped/disabled.
- **Print & Register (batch).** Double-click **`print-register.bat`**, pick multiple
  files, give each its own number (or a prefix + auto-increment), **Print all** —
  each PDF is printed in turn with its own number.

> How it works: `upload.py` runs as SYSTEM, so for the live prompt it launches
> `prompt-id.ps1` **into your interactive session** (`WTSQueryUserToken` +
> `CreateProcessAsUser`). Every mechanism writes a small file under the (locked)
> install folder's user-writable `ids\` subfolder; the uploader reads it, attaches
> the value, and consumes it. It's fully best-effort — if the prompt can't show,
> the print still uploads.

## Encrypting the PDFs (optional, AES-256)

Off by default. When enabled, every PDF is encrypted with **AES-256** — the
strongest standard document encryption — with **no quality loss** (it's encrypted,
not recompressed). It's done by **qpdf** (installed alongside the other tools);
Ghostscript isn't used for this because its encryption is weak (RC4 only).

**Per printer (recommended):** the password is set right when the printer is
added (hub mode install prompt). Manage it later with:
- **`change-password.bat`** — change or disable ONE printer's password (asks for
  the **super-admin password** first; stored **DPAPI-encrypted**, never plaintext).
- **`view-password.bat`** — forgot a printer's password? It is shown on screen
  after super-admin verification. The factory super-admin password is NOT stored
  anywhere in this repo (only its PBKDF2-SHA256 hash is embedded); it is
  distributed to deployment admins out-of-band — **rotate it immediately** with
  **`set-super-admin.bat`**.

**Global fallback:** double-click **`set-password.bat`** (elevates), type a
passphrase (blank = off). It enables encryption for all printers without their
own password, stores the passphrase, and ensures qpdf is installed — no config
editing, no spooler restart.

<sub>Advanced alternatives: a **machine** env var `setx VCP_PDF_PASSWORD "…" /M`
(the `/M` is required — SYSTEM only sees *machine* vars — then `fix-queue.bat` to
restart the spooler), or `config.json` → `pdf_encryption`.</sub>

Received PDFs then need the passphrase to open. **Decode** with **`decode-pdf.bat`**
(pick/drag files, enter the password → `<name>-decrypted.pdf`) or any PDF reader.
To confirm it worked: a PDF printed *after* enabling should prompt for a password
when opened, and `log.txt` shows `Encrypted PDF with AES-256`.

> Fail-safe: if enabled but the passphrase or qpdf is missing, the job is kept in
> `failed\` rather than sent unencrypted. Strength depends on the passphrase — use a
> long random one and store it in a secrets manager.

## Configuration — `config.json`

Located at `%ProgramData%\VirtualCloudPrinter\config.json`. The installer manages
it for you, but you can edit it directly (no reinstall needed — changes apply to
the next print job).

```jsonc
{
  "printers": {
    "Invoices": {                       // MUST match the Windows printer name
      "url": "https://api.example.com/invoices",
      "docname_field": "docname",       // form field name for the document name
      "file_field": "file",             // form field name for the PDF
      "extra_fields": { "source": "erp" },  // any extra form fields to send
      "headers": { "Authorization": "Bearer XYZ" },  // any extra HTTP headers
      "verify_tls": true                // set false only for self-signed test certs
    }
  },

  "default_url": "",                    // used if a printer isn't listed above
  "timeout_seconds": 60,
  "retry_count": 2,                     // retries on upload failure
  "retry_delay_seconds": 3,
  "ghostscript_path": "C:\\Program Files\\gs\\gs10.03.1\\bin\\gswin64c.exe"
}
```

### What the server receives
An HTTP **POST** with `Content-Type: multipart/form-data` containing:
- **`docname`** — the document name Windows assigned to the job (e.g. `Invoice 42`).
- **`file`** — the PDF, sent with filename `<docname>.pdf` and type `application/pdf`.

(The field names are configurable per printer via `docname_field` / `file_field`.)

---

## How it works (under the hood)

1. The printer uses an inbox **v3 PostScript driver** (`MS Publisher Color Printer`),
   so every job becomes **PostScript**.
2. A redirection **port** (registered under the **mfilemon / clawmon** port monitor)
   receives that PostScript, writes it to `spool\`, and launches **`upload.py`**,
   passing the spool file, job id, **printer name** (`%r`), user (`%u`) and
   **document name** (`%t`).
3. `upload.py` (running as SYSTEM) optionally prompts for a registration number,
   runs **Ghostscript** to turn the PostScript into a PDF, looks up the printer's
   URL in `config.json`, and **POSTs** the PDF.
4. On success the temp files are deleted; on failure the PDF is kept in `failed\`
   and the error is written to `log.txt` so nothing is ever lost.

> The spooler runs the port's I/O and launches `upload.py` **while impersonating
> the print-submitting user**, so `install.bat` grants that user the minimal
> access it needs (create the `.ps` in `spool\`, read+execute the interpreter and
> `prompt-id.ps1`) — while keeping `config.json`/`upload.py` locked to
> SYSTEM+Admins. See the security notes below.

---

## Using clawmon instead of mfilemon (optional)

You chose *clawmon* — but clawSoft publishes **no prebuilt clawmon binaries**, so a
one-click installer would have to compile C++ from source. Its parent **mfilemon**
is byte-for-byte compatible (same registry format, same `%t`/`%r`/`%f` macros) and
ships a ready installer, so the installer uses mfilemon by default.

If you specifically want clawmon, build it (Visual Studio) or obtain
`clawmon.dll`, `clawmonui.dll`, and `regmon.exe`, drop them into the **`vendor\`**
folder next to `install.bat`, and re-run `install.bat`. The installer detects them
and registers clawmon automatically. Source: <https://github.com/hessandrew/clawmon>.

---

## Troubleshooting

- **First thing to try: `fix-queue.bat`** (self-elevating). It clears a stuck print
  queue, re-applies the ACLs the print pipeline needs, refreshes the SYSTEM-side
  scripts, and writes a full diagnostic report to **`vcp-diagnostics.txt`** in the
  repo (interpreter check, ACLs, `config.json`, `log.txt` tail, `failed\`).
- **Nothing arrives at my URL.** Open `status.bat` and read the tail of the log, or
  open `%ProgramData%\VirtualCloudPrinter\log.txt`. Each job writes a block there.
- **Jobs stuck in the queue as "Error".** Run `fix-queue.bat` — a poison job can
  only be cleared by restarting the spooler (needs admin), which it does for you.
- **No registration dialog.** Ensure `"prompt_registration": true`, that you're
  logged in interactively, and check `log.txt` for a `Registration prompt: …` line.
- **`Ghostscript not found`.** Set the full path to `gswin64c.exe` in
  `config.json` → `ghostscript_path`.
- **Failed uploads.** Check `%ProgramData%\VirtualCloudPrinter\failed\` — every PDF
  that couldn't be uploaded is preserved there.
- **Want to see errors live?** Temporarily change the port's `UserCommand` from
  `pythonw.exe` to `python.exe`, or just run a job and watch `log.txt`.
- **SmartScreen warning** when the mfilemon installer runs: it's an older signed
  community tool; approve it once, or pre-install mfilemon yourself.

---

## Security notes

- The install folder `%ProgramData%\VirtualCloudPrinter` is locked down to
  **SYSTEM + Administrators** during install. This protects any auth tokens you put
  in a printer's `headers` and prevents a local privilege-escalation vector (the
  uploader runs as SYSTEM).
- The spooler runs the port's I/O and launches `upload.py` **while impersonating
  the print-submitting user**, so the installer grants that user the *minimal*
  extra access it needs: create-only in `spool\` (each user owns only its own job
  file), and **read+execute** on the interpreter and `prompt-id.ps1`. No write is
  granted anywhere it could plant code, and `config.json` / `upload.py` / `log.txt`
  / `failed\` stay SYSTEM+Admins-only.
- Because of that lock-down, edit `config.json` from an **elevated** editor
  (Run as administrator), or via `add-printer.bat`.
- Ghostscript runs with `-dSAFER`. Jobs are only ever sent to the URL configured
  for the printer that received them (matched case/space-insensitively); if a
  printer isn't in `config.json` and no `default_url` is set, the job is preserved
  in `failed\` rather than sent anywhere.

## Files in this project

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `LAN-SETUP.md` | **LAN → LIMS deployment runbook (hub + Supabase) — start here** |
| `SETUP.md` | full clone-to-running walkthrough (single machine / legacy mode) |
| `ARCHITECTURE.md` | interface contracts between client / hub / LIMS (for developers) |
| `install.bat` | one-click install via the GUI wizard (elevates) |
| `add-printer.bat` | add another printer via the GUI wizard (elevates) |
| `setup-gui.ps1` | the click-through setup wizard (launched by the two above) |
| `set-id.bat` / `set-id.ps1` | set a registration number / UUID for your next single print |
| `print-register.bat` / `print-register.ps1` | batch: pick files, give each its own number, print all |
| `prompt-id.ps1` | the per-print registration/test dialog (launched by `upload.py`) |
| `set-password.bat` | set/clear the GLOBAL AES-256 encryption passphrase (elevates) |
| `change-password.bat` | change ONE printer's PDF password (super-admin gated, DPAPI) |
| `view-password.bat` | show a printer's PDF password (super-admin gated) |
| `set-super-admin.bat` | rotate the super-admin password (PBKDF2 hash only) |
| `decode-pdf.bat` / `decode-pdf.ps1` | decrypt an AES-256 encrypted PDF (qpdf/Ghostscript) |
| `status.bat` | show monitor, printers, device names/types, URLs, recent log |
| `fix-queue.bat` | unjam the print queue + write full diagnostics (elevates) |
| `uninstall.bat` | remove printers, port, files, and shortcuts |
| `setup.ps1` | all the actual logic (called by the .bat files) |
| `upload.py` | the per-job converter + uploader (stdlib only) |
| `config.template.json` | starting configuration |
| `test_server.py` | minimal local receiver for end-to-end testing |
| `hub/` | **LIMS Print Hub** — the central LAN receiver/filer/forwarder (FastAPI) |
| `lims/` | cloud LIMS: Supabase schema + React web app for testers |
| `vendor/` | drop `mfilemon-setup.exe` (offline) or clawmon binaries here |
| `simulator/` | FastAPI receiver simulator (legacy dev testing) |
| `COMPLIANCE-AND-PRIVACY.md` | licensing, regulatory & privacy guidance (read for pharma use) |
| `NOTICE` / `LICENSE` | third-party license obligations / this project's MIT license |
